        \State \Return \textsc{U-collapse} \Comment{moderate decline, no V-shape}
    \EndIf
\Else \Comment{$\Delta > \tau$}
    \State $g_1 \gets a_{25} - a_{0}$ \Comment{first-half gain}
    \State $g_2 \gets a_{100} - a_{25}$ \Comment{second-half gain}
    \State $\ell \gets a_{100} - a_{50}$ \Comment{late gain}
    \If{$(g_1 > 0$ \textbf{and} $g_2 < \rho \cdot g_1)$ \textbf{or} $\ell < \lambda$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{saturating gain}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{sustained gain}
    \EndIf
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

ContamCal operates in two stages: Stage~1 estimates per-item contamination exposure from multiple detection signals via training-free fusion; Stage~2 embeds these scores in a contamination-augmented IRT model to produce calibrated ability estimates with credible intervals.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

Let $y_{ijk} \in \{0,1\}$ denote the binary response of model~$j$ ($j=1,\ldots,J$) to item~$i$ ($i=1,\ldots,I_k$) on benchmark~$k$. Standard IRT assumes $P(y_{ijk}=1) = f(\theta_j, \boldsymbol{\xi}_i)$ where $\theta_j$ is latent ability and $\boldsymbol{\xi}_i$ collects item parameters. When training corpora contain benchmark items, the observed ability $\hat{\theta}_j$ confounds genuine capability with memorization.

Our goal is to estimate $\theta_j^{\text{clean}}$, the ability each model would exhibit absent contamination, and to diagnose contamination effects at domain granularity. We adapt item compromise IRT \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge} and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt} for LLM evaluation: replacing binary leakage indicators with continuous exposure scores $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$, fitting per-benchmark models to handle the shared item pool \citep{sanmartin_2009_irt}, and recovering clean ability via counterfactual inference (setting $e_{ij}=0$).

\subsection{Stage 1: Multi-Signal Exposure Mapping}
\label{sec:stage1}

For each model~$j$ and item~$i$, we consider four candidate detection signals. \textbf{S1}~(Embedding similarity): cosine similarity between the item embedding and its nearest neighbor in the SFT training corpus. \textbf{S2}~(Min-K\%++): a membership inference statistic based on token-level surprisal \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp}. \textbf{S3}~(ConStat): the accuracy gap between original and paraphrased item variants \citep{dekoninck2024constat}. \textbf{S4}~(Self-Critique): negative entropy of model self-evaluation distributions, inspired by the finding that LLMs can assess their own knowledge \citep{kadavath2022}; concurrent work by \citet{tao2025detecting} uses a related but mechanistically distinct entropy collapse approach targeting RL-trained models. S3 requires paired paraphrased items as reference and is therefore limited to benchmarks with existing paraphrase sets. Our primary pipeline uses the remaining three signals ($\mathcal{S} = \{\text{S1, S2, S4}\}$) and is applicable to any benchmark without auxiliary data requirements. S2 and S4 exhibit known failure modes under RL post-training, which we characterize in \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}.

We aggregate signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep[RRF;][]{cormack_2009_rrf}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rrf}
    e_{ij} = \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \frac{1}{\kappa + r_{s}(i)},
\end{equation}
where $r_s(i)$ is the rank of item~$i$ under signal~$s$ (rank~1 = most suspicious), $\kappa=60$ is the standard smoothing constant, and $|\mathcal{S}|=3$ in our primary configuration. Raw scores are min-max normalized per model to obtain $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$. The resulting distribution is highly right-skewed (mean 0.041, median 0.016, max 0.879), with most model-item pairs receiving near-zero exposure.

\subsection{Stage 2: Contamination-Augmented IRT}
\label{sec:stage2}

\paragraph{Primary model: 2PL+$\gamma$.}
Our primary specification augments the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} with a contamination covariate. We fit independent IRT models per benchmark~$k$, each with its own contamination effect $\gamma_k$. Per-benchmark fitting avoids cross-benchmark item parameter borrowing while permitting heterogeneous contamination effects. For benchmark~$k$, the response probability is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2pl_gamma}
    P(y_{ijk} = 1) = \sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i) + \gamma_k \cdot e_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the logistic function, $a_i > 0$ is item discrimination, $b_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is item difficulty, $\theta_j$ is model ability, and $\gamma_k$ is the scalar contamination effect for benchmark~$k$. Each benchmark is fitted independently; when finer-grained diagnosis is needed, we further stratify within a benchmark by cognitive domain (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

The additive structure admits a natural interpretation: contamination shifts the effective difficulty of item~$i$ by $\gamma_k \cdot e_{ij} / a_i$ units on the ability scale (this holds exactly under linearization of the logistic function; the true shift is nonlinear and probability-dependent).

For MC-format benchmarks, a 4PL extension adds guessing and ceiling asymptotes (Appendix~\ref{sec:4pl_details}); this requires $J \geq 16$ and is not used in domain-level analyses ($J = 13$).

\paragraph{Inference.}
We perform Bayesian inference via NUTS \citep{hoffman2014} in Stan \citep{carpenter2017} with four chains and target acceptance 0.95. Priors: $\theta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $a_i \sim \text{LogNormal}(0, 0.5)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $\gamma_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Convergence requires split-$\hat{R} < 1.01$ and bulk ESS~$> 400$ for all parameters.

\paragraph{Calibrated score.}
The decontaminated accuracy for model~$j$ on benchmark~$k$ is the posterior expectation under zero exposure:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:calibrated}
    \text{Acc}_{j}^{\text{cal}} = \frac{1}{I_k} \sum_{i=1}^{I_k}\, \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi} \sim p(\boldsymbol{\psi} \mid \mathbf{y})} \!\left[\sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i)\bigr)\right],
\end{equation}
with credible intervals from posterior quantiles. Models with noisier exposure signals receive wider intervals by construction.

\paragraph{Identifiability.}
\label{sec:fisher}
\label{sec:value_decomposition}
The reliability of $\gamma$ depends on signal AUC and ensemble size $J$; simulation validates that recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability}). Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated} depends on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma$ directly, predicting that vanilla 2PL ($\gamma\!=\!0$) achieves comparable calibration to 2PL+$\gamma$, with $\gamma_k$ serving diagnosis rather than calibration (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

\subsection{Dose-Response Profile Classification}
\label{sec:classification}

Independent of the IRT calibration pipeline, we formalize how each model's accuracy trajectory across dosage levels is classified into a canonical dose-response pattern. Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} defines a deterministic decision tree over the accuracy vector $\mathbf{a} = (a_0, a_5, a_{25}, a_{50}, a_{100})$, parameterized by a noise threshold $\tau$, severity threshold $\sigma$, saturation ratio $\rho$, and late-gain floor $\lambda$. The three-way top-level split (immune/harm/benefit) is governed by the sign and magnitude of $\Delta = a_{100} - a_0$ relative to $\tau$; within-branch refinements distinguish U-collapse from V-recovery (via mid-dosage trough detection); within the benefit branch, gain trajectory shape (monotonic vs.\ saturating) is logged but both map to a single Benefit pattern. A mid-trajectory volatility check ($v^*$) within the immune branch distinguishes true immunity (Phi-4: $v^* = 0.34$pp $\le \tau$) from V-recovery with full endpoint recovery (Qwen-7B seed$=$123: $\Delta \approx 0$ but $v^* = 3.22$pp $> \tau$); recovery extent varies by seed (partial at seed$=$42, full at seed$=$123). All thresholds are explicit and adjustable; a 30-combination grid sweep confirms all families are stable across all $(\tau, \sigma)$ combinations (180/180 model--seed--threshold pairs; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Dose-Response Profile Classification}
\label{alg:profile-classification}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Accuracy vector $\mathbf{a} = (a_{0}, a_{5}, a_{25}, a_{50}, a_{100})$ at dosages $d \in \{0, 5, 25, 50, 100\}\%$
\Require Noise threshold $\tau = 1$ pp, severity threshold $\sigma = 5$ pp
\Require Saturation ratio $\rho = 0.3$, late-gain floor $\lambda = 0.5$ pp
\Ensure Pattern $p \in \{\textsc{Immune}, \textsc{Collapse}, \textsc{V-recovery}, \textsc{Benefit}\}$
\State $\Delta \gets a_{100} - a_{0}$ \Comment{end-to-end accuracy shift}
\State $m^* \gets \min(a_{5},\, a_{25},\, a_{50})$ \Comment{mid-dosage minimum}
\State $v^* \gets \max_{i \in \{5,25,50\}} |a_i - a_0|$ \Comment{max mid-trajectory deviation}
\If{$|\Delta| \le \tau$}
    \If{$v^* \le \tau$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Immune} \Comment{fluctuation within noise band}
    \ElsIf{$m^* < a_{0}$ \textbf{and} $m^* < a_{100}$}
        \State \Return \textsc{V-recovery} \Comment{V-dip with full endpoint recovery}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Immune} \Comment{non-V volatility, endpoint stable}
    \EndIf
\ElsIf{$\Delta < -\tau$}
    \If{$\Delta \le -\sigma$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Collapse} \Comment{severe monotonic degradation}
    \ElsIf{$m^* < a_{0}$ \textbf{and} $m^* < a_{100}$}
        \State \Return \textsc{V-recovery} \Comment{mid-dosage trough with partial recovery}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Collapse} \Comment{moderate decline, no V-shape}
    \EndIf
\Else \Comment{$\Delta > \tau$}
    \State $g_1 \gets a_{25} - a_{0}$ \Comment{first-half gain}
    \State $g_2 \gets a_{100} - a_{25}$ \Comment{second-half gain}
    \State $\ell \gets a_{100} - a_{50}$ \Comment{late gain}
    \If{$(g_1 > 0$ \textbf{and} $g_2 < \rho \cdot g_1)$ \textbf{or} $\ell < \lambda$}
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{saturating gain}
    \Else
        \State \Return \textsc{Benefit} \Comment{sustained gain}
    \EndIf
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into five models spanning four independent families from four labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google) and discover qualitatively different dose-response patterns---from immunity (Phi-4 14.7B, $\pm$0.5pp on MMLU) to catastrophic U-type collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$10pp). Contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band: below it, models benefit (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); above it, they are harmed or immune. Counter-intuitively, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. Using ContamCal, a two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), we provide the statistical machinery for this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dose-response taxonomy with cross-seed and cross-benchmark validation.} Controlled injection across four families reveals qualitatively different dose-response patterns; contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band, while models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Cross-benchmark transfer to GSM8K reveals three distinct patterns (null, consistent, directional reversal), further demonstrating family-specific contamination dynamics. Multi-seed evaluation shows effect direction is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive, underscoring the necessity of reporting confidence intervals. Within-family domain analysis reveals additional heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{Global modulation, not memorization.} Difference-in-differences analysis on 14{,}042 MMLU items shows no differential accuracy gain for contaminated vs.\ clean items in any model family, providing causal evidence that SFT contamination operates through global capability modulation rather than item-specific memorization.
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ to $-1.98$). RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We identify ten limitations that bound the scope of our conclusions.

\paragraph{Scale ceiling.}
All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters. Larger models (70B+) may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without producing measurable signal divergence.

\paragraph{Seed sensitivity.}
Cross-seed analysis (\S\ref{sec:cross_seed}) confirms that effect \emph{direction} is reproducible across three seeds, but magnitude and dose-response shape exhibit seed-dependent variability. OLMo-2's cross-seed CI crosses zero ($p=0.039$), rendering its benefit classification borderline. Practitioners should report multi-seed CIs rather than single-seed point estimates.

\paragraph{SFT ecological validity.}
We use LoRA-based SFT injection exclusively in AUGMENT mode (contaminated items appended to, not replacing, base SFT data). Pretraining-stage leakage, which occurs during large-scale web crawl ingestion, may produce qualitatively different contamination signatures. The position bias at 0.5B (A-option rate escalating from 46\% to 98\% with dosage) is likely SFT-specific. Generalization to pretraining contamination requires further validation.

\paragraph{ARC-Challenge bootstrap stability.}
ARC-Challenge contains only 1{,}172 items (vs.\ 14{,}042 for MMLU), resulting in substantially lower bootstrap profile stability: OLMo-2 53.3\%, Gemma-2 62.7\% (vs.\ $\geq$76.6\% on MMLU). Gemma-2's ARC $\Delta$acc 95\% CI crosses zero, and Qwen-14B exhibits a different pattern (V-recovery) on ARC than on MMLU (U-collapse). Cross-benchmark consistency claims should be interpreted as directional agreement, not pattern-level replication.

\paragraph{Signal coverage.}
S1 (embedding similarity) requires training corpus access, unavailable for closed-source models. For API-only models, only S2 and S4 are applicable, and both show poor discrimination below 1.5B (\S\ref{sec:signal_failure}).

\paragraph{Ensemble size and per-domain power.}
Reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$. Our domain-level analysis ($J = 13$--$16$) yields a strong directional signal for only one of eight MMLU domains. Per-domain $\gamma$ diagnosis at current ensemble sizes is exploratory.

\paragraph{Position bias.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits position bias correlated with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100); these models are excluded from MC IRT analyses, reducing ensemble size.

\paragraph{Model misfit in non-STEM domains.}
A single linear $\gamma$ cannot capture non-monotone contamination effects within a domain. The dose-response taxonomy operates at the model level; richer within-domain specifications may be required for domain-level calibration.

\paragraph{Additive $\gamma$ linearity.}
The 2PL+$\gamma$ model assumes contamination effects are additive and linear in exposure. PPP posterior predictive checks cannot detect threshold-type model misspecification (PPP remains 0.50--0.56 despite up to 33\% $\gamma$ overestimation), which highlights the value of simulation-based specification testing. The threshold DGP simulation uses uniform exposure; real RRF exposure is right-skewed (mean$=$0.041), so the quantitative overestimation magnitude may differ, but the qualitative direction (overestimation, not attenuation) is robust.

\paragraph{Prompt format sensitivity.}
The observed dose-response profiles are conditional on the evaluation protocol (5-shot prompting for MMLU, 25-shot for ARC-Challenge). Preliminary comparison with 0-shot evaluation suggests that pattern shapes may vary with prompt format---for instance, the V-type recovery pattern observed under 5-shot prompting can disappear under 0-shot conditions. This indicates that contamination effects interact with in-context learning mechanisms. While internal consistency is maintained (all 75 model--dosage configurations use the same protocol), practitioners replicating our analyses under different prompting regimes should expect quantitatively and potentially qualitatively different dose-response profiles. Future work should systematically evaluate protocol sensitivity across shot counts and prompt templates.

